# Observations to an inert proposal with an explicit unit callback

## Goal

Turn two Kelvin temperature observations of a Thing into a Celsius Nx batch,
run a small `defn`, and decode the result as an inert `setTarget` Action
proposal that nothing dispatches. The convenience call
`Wotex.Lab.Examples.Thermal.run/0` is shown next to every public `wotex_nx`
call it makes, so a consumer can copy the pipeline without the Lab.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.13.1"}
])
```

The requirements above are published-artifact requirements. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`priv/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout. No native Nx backend,
model download or server is needed: `Nx.BinaryBackend` and
`Nx.Defn.Evaluator` are selected explicitly.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TD parsing and DataSchema | `wotex` | `Wotex.ThingDescription`, `Wotex.DataSchema` |
| Observation, row, feature, schema, encoder, decoder, output schema | `wotex_nx` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Nx.*` |
| Unit conversion policy (`K` to `Cel` only) | `wotex_lab` (consumer-implements `Wotex.Nx.UnitConverter`) | `Wotex.Lab.Adapters.Nx.UnitConverter` |
| The `defn`, backend and compiler selection | consumer | `Wotex.Lab.Examples.Thermal.target/1` here |
| Any effect | nobody in this notebook | the proposal stays inert |

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.{DataSchema, ThingDescription}
alias Wotex.Lab.Adapters.Nx.UnitConverter
alias Wotex.Lab.Examples.Thermal
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry
alias Wotex.Nx.{Decoder, Encoded, Encoder, Feature, Observation, OutputSchema, Row, Schema}

telemetry_id = "cookbook-thermal-nx-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())
```

The convenience path first: one call, a TD, an encoded batch and a proposal.

```elixir
{:ok, convenience} = Thermal.run()
{Encoded.feature_order(convenience.encoded), convenience.proposal}
```

Now the same pipeline written out with the package calls. The fixture TD
supplies the Thing id, the temperature DataSchema and the Action input schema.

```elixir
path = Application.app_dir(:wotex_lab, "priv/fixtures/thermal/thing-description.json")
{:ok, td} = path |> File.read!() |> ThingDescription.parse()
document = ThingDescription.to_map(td)
thing_id = ThingDescription.id(td)

{:ok, input_schema} = DataSchema.new(Map.take(document["properties"]["temperature"], ["type", "unit"]))
{:ok, output_schema} = DataSchema.new(document["actions"]["setTarget"]["input"])

{:ok, feature} =
  Feature.new(
    name: "temperature",
    thing_id: thing_id,
    affordance_type: :property,
    affordance_name: "temperature",
    data_schema: input_schema,
    accepted_quality: [:good],
    missing: :error
  )

{:ok, schema} = Schema.new(features: [feature], max_rows: 2)
```

Observations carry their own unit; the encoder asks the explicit converter
to reach the feature's `Cel` schema and revalidates the result.

```elixir
observe = fn id, at, kelvin ->
  {:ok, observation} =
    Observation.new(
      id: id,
      thing_id: thing_id,
      affordance_type: :property,
      affordance_name: "temperature",
      observed_at: at,
      value: kelvin,
      unit: "K",
      quality: :good
    )

  {:ok, row} = Row.new(at, %{"temperature" => observation})
  row
end

rows = [observe.("thermal-1", 0, 293.15), observe.("thermal-2", 1_000, 295.15)]

{:ok, encoded} =
  Nx.with_default_backend(Nx.BinaryBackend, fn ->
    Encoder.encode(rows, schema, unit_converter: {UnitConverter, []})
  end)

{{values}, {masks}, quality} =
  Nx.Defn.jit_apply(&Function.identity/1, [Encoded.batch(encoded)], compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)

%{
  feature_order: Encoded.feature_order(encoded),
  timestamps: Encoded.timestamps(encoded),
  provenance: Encoded.provenance(encoded),
  values: Nx.to_flat_list(values),
  masks: Nx.to_flat_list(masks),
  quality: Nx.to_flat_list(quality)
}
```

The numerical step is the Lab's `target/1` `defn` (mask-weighted mean plus
one degree) run through the evaluator; any consumer function with the same
container shape works.

```elixir
tensor =
  Telemetry.span(:nx, :inference, %{profile: :cookbook}, fn ->
    Nx.Defn.jit_apply(&Thermal.target/1, [Encoded.batch(encoded)], compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)
  end)

{:ok, output} =
  OutputSchema.new(
    kind: :action_proposal,
    thing_id: thing_id,
    affordance_type: :action,
    affordance_name: "setTarget",
    data_schema: output_schema,
    dtype: :f32
  )

{:ok, proposal} = Decoder.decode(tensor, output, id: "thermal-proposal-1", proposed_at: 2_000)
proposal
```

## Deliberate breakage

Three inputs that must never become a proposal: a unit the converter does
not know, an observation from another Thing, and a model output outside the
Action's schema bounds.

```elixir
{:ok, fahrenheit} =
  Observation.new(
    id: "sample",
    thing_id: thing_id,
    affordance_type: :property,
    affordance_name: "temperature",
    observed_at: 0,
    value: 68,
    unit: "F"
  )

{:ok, fahrenheit_row} = Row.new(0, %{"temperature" => fahrenheit})

{:error, unit_error} =
  Encoder.encode([fahrenheit_row], schema, unit_converter: {UnitConverter, []})

{:ok, stranger} =
  Observation.new(
    id: "stranger",
    thing_id: "urn:test:2",
    affordance_type: :property,
    affordance_name: "temperature",
    observed_at: 0,
    value: 20.0,
    unit: "Cel"
  )

{:ok, stranger_row} = Row.new(0, %{"temperature" => stranger})
{:error, identity_error} = Encoder.encode([stranger_row], schema)

{:error, range_error} =
  Decoder.decode(Nx.tensor(40.0, type: :f32), output, id: "too-hot", proposed_at: 0)

{unit_error.phase, identity_error.code, range_error.code}
```

## Safe telemetry

The Lab spans name the component and operation; labels are the profile and
the outcome only. Tensor contents and the Thing id never appear.

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

labels =
  for {[:wotex, :lab, component, operation, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- spans,
      do: {component, operation, metadata}

labels_safe? =
  Enum.all?(labels, fn {_component, _operation, metadata} ->
    Enum.all?(Map.values(metadata), &(is_atom(&1) and not (is_binary(&1) and &1 =~ "urn:")))
  end) and not (inspect(labels) =~ "urn:wotex")

{Enum.map(labels, &{elem(&1, 0), elem(&1, 1)}), labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `feature-order` | `["temperature"]` with timestamps `[0, 1000]` |
| `observed-masks` | masks `[1, 1]`, quality `[0, 0]`, values about `[20.0, 22.0]` |
| `proposal-input` | `setTarget` for the fixture Thing with input about `22.0` at `2000` |
| `convenience-agrees` | `Thermal.run/0` produced the same proposal |
| `unit-refused` | Fahrenheit fails in phase `:unit` |
| `identity-refused` | Another Thing's observation is refused by the encoder |
| `output-range-refused` | `40.0` is outside `[5, 35]` and is not decoded |

```elixir
close? = fn left, right -> abs(left - right) < 0.00001 end

%{
  "feature-order" =>
    Encoded.feature_order(encoded) == ["temperature"] and Encoded.timestamps(encoded) == [0, 1_000],
  "observed-masks" =>
    Nx.to_flat_list(masks) == [1, 1] and Nx.to_flat_list(quality) == [0, 0] and
      Enum.zip(Nx.to_flat_list(values), [20.0, 22.0]) |> Enum.all?(fn {a, b} -> close?.(a, b) end),
  "proposal-input" =>
    proposal.thing_id == thing_id and proposal.action_name == "setTarget" and
      proposal.proposed_at == 2_000 and close?.(proposal.input, 22.0),
  "convenience-agrees" => convenience.proposal == proposal,
  "unit-refused" => unit_error.phase == :unit,
  "identity-refused" => match?(%Wotex.Nx.Error{}, identity_error),
  "output-range-refused" => match?(%Wotex.Nx.Error{}, range_error) and labels_safe?
}
```

## Public seam

`nx.unit_converter` — `Wotex.Nx.UnitConverter.convert/5` (consumer-implements;
the Lab reference handles `K` and `Cel` only). `core.td` for the parse.

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.03 `thermal-nx` lane (`WLB-C03`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `WNX-C03`, `WNX-C04` (`wotex_nx:WNX.01`).

## Replacement adapter

Implement `Wotex.Nx.UnitConverter` in your own module and pass it as
`unit_converter: {YourConverter, config}` to `Wotex.Nx.Encoder.encode/3`.
Return `{:ok, number}` only for conversions you can justify; return
`{:error, reason}` for everything else. The encoder still validates the
converted value against the feature's DataSchema, so a converter cannot
smuggle an out-of-schema value into a tensor.
